= Pool

== Lane 1

// `->l` specifies that the sequence flow continues
// at another node with a matching `<-l`.
// NB: The `l` is _not_ a node matcher.
# Start ->l

== Lane 2

// Here is the matching `<-l`. In this example
// the arrows are used to cross from one to another lane.
// Usually the
// Add edge text after `->l` or `<-l`.
. End <-l Optional Edge Text

= Another Pool

== Lane 1
// You can reuse labels across pools without interference.
// In this case `l` is used in two pools, but BPMD knows
// that they are not connected (you must use MF for
// inter-pool connections).
# Start ->l
. End <-l2

== Lane 2
// You can continue to jump by using both `<-` and `->`
// on the same node.
- Next <-l ->l2
